description "Wait for a DOS guest to be able to take a command"
platform dos
machine  running
timeout  2m

# **Readiness is testaferro's to define, not the provider's.**
# Reliquary ships no readiness script deliberately: what "ready"
# means belongs to whatever is being built. For a guest suite it
# means one thing — the guest will accept a command — and the
# evidence is a command prompt it drew itself.
#
# The prompt is matched as a pattern rather than a literal because
# the letter is not fixed: testaferro's own installed system boots
# to C:, while a tester's boot floppy (U3) boots to A:. Matching
# the shape rather than the drive keeps one script honest for both.
wait /[A-Z]:\\?[^>]*>/

# The last step, and the whole point of the script. A machine
# variable is the script's channel back to the host, and it is
# cleared at every start — so finding it set says *this* boot got
# here, never a previous one.
set ready "yes"
